WEBVTT

00:02.906 --> 00:09.170
I don't know how much Sean has told you about... Not very much.

00:09.190 --> 00:11.692
You mentioned that you were...

00:12.212 --> 00:22.460
He was surprised in some cases, but interested in my background.

00:24.681 --> 00:28.123
And said, well, I know somebody who would like to talk to you.

00:28.644 --> 00:29.564
And then...

00:31.248 --> 00:34.269
You mentioned in your email what your phone is.

00:34.289 --> 00:34.769
Yeah.

00:36.629 --> 00:39.670
I'm just, I'd like to do background on people I'm interviewing.

00:39.690 --> 00:44.571
So I did a little digging, and just to see that you worked here in the 90s, is that right?

00:45.771 --> 00:46.952
Did you work here in the 90s?

00:47.052 --> 00:48.052
Is that what the term is?

00:48.072 --> 00:50.312
Yeah, I came 1985, and...

00:53.746 --> 00:58.948
Retired the first one in 2000, then they called me back.

00:59.468 --> 01:01.829
It's like bad cars, they get recalled.

01:01.889 --> 01:04.470
So I retired again in 2002 and again in 2003.

01:04.510 --> 01:05.290
You can't stay away.

01:13.470 --> 01:17.735
Well, my replacement kept either quitting or not coming.

01:18.255 --> 01:19.977
Yeah, I know.

01:20.498 --> 01:29.388
So, and at that time, in fact, that's what I came for, is they had...

01:30.870 --> 01:46.985
the beginning of a separate telecommunication program, separate from electronic, the common base for, I guess, a freshman and sophomore, and then they branch out into a telecom.

01:47.325 --> 01:51.769
And my role was to develop the telecom part.

02:01.469 --> 02:07.134
When they did call me back, I said, well, I'm retired.

02:07.154 --> 02:08.015
They said, yeah.

02:16.279 --> 02:16.940
Okay.

02:17.060 --> 02:18.161
That's a tricky situation.

02:18.181 --> 02:19.882
It's like they got you there.

02:19.902 --> 02:22.925
They know which button to push.

02:23.386 --> 02:25.247
Very much.

02:27.729 --> 02:28.830
I wasn't forced to.

02:29.511 --> 02:30.652
Right.

02:32.794 --> 02:34.115
So I'm going to be honest.

02:34.215 --> 02:34.416
I know

02:35.597 --> 02:47.440
about technology, I'm not very technologically savvy, so I was doing a little background research too into your field, and it very quickly turned into another foreign language to me.

02:48.880 --> 03:00.963
But it was so interesting seeing how you were part of the great technological shift on the university, like you were part of that, you were there, you saw that.

03:04.048 --> 03:11.452
that period of time telecom was booming and the technology was also moving so very fast.

03:12.513 --> 03:27.140
But we actually got after the third year a separate accreditation for the telecom program, EBIT accreditation, so that was good.

03:27.300 --> 03:28.661
Yeah, absolutely.

03:29.301 --> 03:32.663
We were, I think,

03:34.212 --> 03:40.572
The second strictly telecom program, as opposed to electronic, electricity.

03:42.513 --> 03:45.894
and the first undergraduate to get accredited.

03:46.375 --> 03:47.895
So that was good.

03:47.915 --> 03:48.736
Yeah, absolutely.

03:49.876 --> 03:54.878
How was that, sorry that I'm jumping right into this, but how was that process of getting accredited?

03:55.018 --> 03:56.219
Like, what did that entail?

03:56.479 --> 04:00.061
Oh, that's the very long process.

04:00.201 --> 04:07.364
It's a lot, you know, there's this much paperwork.

04:07.504 --> 04:10.345
They want to know all the description of it,

04:10.738 --> 04:19.305
program, how many people, students do you have, how many graduates, how many are employed, etc.

04:19.765 --> 04:26.731
But each course, we get a detail, not just a summary, they want to see everything on the course.

04:27.631 --> 04:30.794
It's a very long preparation process.

04:31.374 --> 04:34.617
And, you know, everybody has to do it, but

04:39.684 --> 04:44.366
Does that go to like, whenever you finish that, does that go to like a national type of?

04:44.466 --> 04:44.726
Yes.

04:45.466 --> 04:58.552
And so you submit that, and so professors of other universities that have a telecom program or something related or are professionally involved in telecom,

04:59.032 --> 05:07.021
review all of these and then decided, yeah, let's go visit or not.

05:07.701 --> 05:18.913
And so there's a group of three or four people that come and spend two or three days here looking at the labs, looking at the talking, interviewing the students.

05:20.915 --> 05:24.197
It's a good process, but it takes a lot of time.

05:24.517 --> 05:26.678
Yeah, I could definitely see that.

05:28.159 --> 05:31.662
I would love to get you in touch with this gentleman named Mark Baggett.

05:32.462 --> 05:39.246
He does the technological services for the library, but he has access to the supercomputers that we have here.

05:40.187 --> 05:44.950
So I was like, I feel like that's something that... Well, that's a different orientation.

05:44.970 --> 05:45.170
Yeah.

05:45.410 --> 05:45.811
Yeah.

05:48.785 --> 05:51.806
But can I rewind and go back a little bit?

05:51.906 --> 05:55.588
Can you tell me about where you were born and where you were raised?

05:55.608 --> 06:04.091
I was born in Paris, France, just before World War II, 1937.

06:05.271 --> 06:17.376
And we had to, because of the German army invasion of France, we had to move

06:18.920 --> 06:31.586
I think three or four times during that period to, well, it's, I was just a kid.

06:31.666 --> 06:38.569
I was a little kid, so, but I do remember suddenly, no, we gotta go.

06:38.789 --> 06:41.390
And it was not like,

06:47.367 --> 06:48.769
Who was in your family at that time?

06:50.270 --> 07:14.550
Well, it's, I was an only child, but my, let's see, yeah, my biological father had to leave because of the war situation.

07:24.129 --> 07:36.494
But later on, I guess, I don't know the details, but my parents divorced.

07:36.774 --> 07:42.396
And again, I know he had gone overseas.

07:44.577 --> 07:45.857
And I don't know the details.

07:45.917 --> 07:47.438
Mother didn't want to talk about it.

07:49.037 --> 07:52.458
But she remarried.

07:53.599 --> 08:06.383
And amazingly, that new father of mine, I mean, he literally adopted me.

08:07.723 --> 08:12.625
And we went through the war.

08:12.705 --> 08:15.206
And he took a chance.

08:20.864 --> 08:31.406
He was, I don't remember which level, co-director of a factory.

08:32.046 --> 08:38.928
And of course, German took that over and so we left again.

08:40.188 --> 08:47.309
So it was, and all the bombardments and this and that.

08:48.390 --> 08:50.610
Can I ask his name and your mom's name?

08:52.334 --> 08:54.015
I don't know if they want that.

08:54.095 --> 08:56.638
Oh, okay, that's fine.

08:58.699 --> 09:02.363
Yeah, I think I'll answer that.

09:02.383 --> 09:03.444
Yeah, absolutely.

09:03.464 --> 09:03.764
Sorry.

09:04.004 --> 09:05.165
No, no, no, that's fine.

09:05.525 --> 09:11.811
Well, actually, my biological father, you can have his first name was Rene, R-E-E.

09:18.475 --> 09:28.112
He had a, from what I read much later on, he had a PhD in marine biology.

09:29.074 --> 09:29.735
Oh, how unique.

09:31.019 --> 09:52.013
And in fact, went to, that was after the war was over, went to New Caledonia, which is a small island in the Pacific, north of New Zealand, way north of it, and set up an aquarium there.

09:52.694 --> 09:57.957
They founded the aquarium and became quite famous in this field.

09:58.477 --> 09:58.717
Wow.

10:00.199 --> 10:07.423
He published three big books.

10:08.303 --> 10:16.988
But I actually only left, that was a year and a half, I think.

10:17.868 --> 10:18.809
And I never met him.

10:20.770 --> 10:24.992
Well, that's on the other side of the world, literally.

10:25.012 --> 10:25.712
Right, yeah.

10:37.740 --> 10:39.960
But your mom, how strong, how strong?

10:40.080 --> 10:41.281
Wow.

10:41.441 --> 10:42.821
Yeah.

10:43.421 --> 10:46.241
She had to face lots of other problems.

10:46.482 --> 10:46.802
Yeah.

10:47.182 --> 10:56.103
Well, the single mother and, you know, the financial input had disappeared, so she had to find a job.

10:57.803 --> 11:03.604
And, yeah, and she has her own Ph.D., Ph.D. in sociology.

11:03.865 --> 11:04.245
Wow.

11:05.345 --> 11:06.565
So I had two...

11:09.361 --> 11:10.562
imposing parents.

11:10.582 --> 11:12.022
Yeah, very educated.

11:14.624 --> 11:17.526
How did you end up in Texas, of all places?

11:17.546 --> 11:22.189
That was a long circuitous study.

11:23.490 --> 11:26.572
I studied in France for high school, of course, and then

11:29.060 --> 11:31.842
junior college, but it was specialized.

11:32.163 --> 11:42.572
It was strictly radio and electronics, except for some civics type.

11:44.393 --> 11:53.781
And then I did my military service in France for three years in their equivalent of the signal corps.

11:53.821 --> 11:54.502
So I was

12:01.483 --> 12:07.287
Since I had done two years of radio school, it made sense for them to put me there.

12:09.468 --> 12:15.833
And after that, I worked in Paris for a year and a half.

12:17.934 --> 12:23.098
And then a friend of mine, who I met in France several times,

12:23.892 --> 12:25.972
said, I had mentioned it.

12:26.773 --> 12:28.993
He said, you know, you need to come and visit us.

12:29.113 --> 12:33.434
And I said, well, yeah, but financialization plus.

12:34.314 --> 12:41.816
He said, well, and then he actually got me a work permit for a year.

12:41.876 --> 12:42.876
So I went there.

12:42.896 --> 12:47.077
And the idea was, well, I'll work and I'll save as much money as possible.

12:47.217 --> 12:48.957
And then I'd play tours.

12:52.641 --> 13:03.787
But it's not the case anymore, but we're talking 1964, 65.

13:04.968 --> 13:12.132
The telecom technology in the US was easily 10 years ahead of the French one.

13:14.473 --> 13:17.795
The telephone network, there was no internet.

13:18.852 --> 13:22.735
digital transmission at that time, but a telephone network in France.

13:23.135 --> 13:30.982
It was not a priority, and the telecom was government-owned, most of Europe.

13:31.202 --> 13:31.922
Oh, I did not know that.

13:32.062 --> 13:35.785
Yeah, almost, yeah, actually all of Europe.

13:37.787 --> 13:42.350
The Postal Service and the telecommunications were government-owned.

13:43.813 --> 14:06.039
And the telephone network, I mean, you know, this is not, it's what, 10 years after the end of World War II, they had a lot of other problems, and their decision was, yeah, we have a telephone network, we'll modernize it later, we gotta use the money.

14:06.899 --> 14:08.380
For other things, yeah.

14:11.184 --> 14:17.807
I discovered that very quickly, that this is fascinating.

14:23.350 --> 14:30.173
I'm a ham radio operator, so I met some American ham.

14:30.193 --> 14:32.594
I was in New Hampshire for that work.

14:37.244 --> 14:41.951
One of them, actually, well,

14:45.625 --> 14:50.029
I told him that my work permit was only expired.

14:50.649 --> 14:57.754
And he said, well, you know, well no.

14:58.495 --> 15:07.381
He had a small repair and installation shop for mobile radios.

15:08.723 --> 15:16.873
And he was specialized in equipping and maintaining radios for fire departments.

15:17.274 --> 15:21.720
Most of them volunteer fire departments around the South New Hampshire.

15:22.781 --> 15:23.402
And he said,

15:25.180 --> 15:33.423
I had the technician who messed up seriously almost six months ago, and I desperately need somebody.

15:33.503 --> 15:34.803
Would you be interested?

15:35.224 --> 15:36.324
Yep.

15:36.584 --> 15:37.885
Well, let's see what I can do.

15:39.762 --> 15:49.906
I guess he convinced the immigration folks that I really need somebody, and I haven't been able to find a small town in New Hampshire.

15:50.746 --> 15:53.447
And so I got another work permit.

15:53.487 --> 15:54.968
That worked out perfectly.

15:55.088 --> 16:04.171
And while I was doing that, I met a young lady who is now my wife, and we've been married for 57 years, I think.

16:04.692 --> 16:05.132
Wow.

16:05.212 --> 16:08.133
So made the right decision, obviously.

16:09.673 --> 16:17.198
So I worked there, and then same thing, you know, one year.

16:18.118 --> 16:29.885
So I went to immigration in Boston and said, is there any way I can renew this?

16:30.365 --> 16:36.549
And I said, no, surely by now I would have been able to find somebody.

16:36.589 --> 16:37.970
Yeah, okay.

16:39.053 --> 17:04.562
And we discussed, and I started the paperwork, and she, I still remember Lizzie went to talk to her boss, I guess, and came back and said, there is one possible, if you can find a teaching job, you can stay, we'll give you a, whatever it's called, it's not the green card, it's

17:06.121 --> 17:13.066
Definitely a work permit and also a temporary status, I guess.

17:13.907 --> 17:16.349
But you can't leave New Hampshire.

17:16.369 --> 17:19.611
I said, okay, I can leave that.

17:20.051 --> 17:23.374
But that was kind of mid-July.

17:23.394 --> 17:28.317
I was like, ooh, school starts in, what, four weeks?

17:28.397 --> 17:28.858
Yeah.

17:30.088 --> 17:32.549
And that was marvelous.

17:32.569 --> 17:36.451
That was the little town in Peterborough, New Hampshire.

17:36.971 --> 17:44.073
Four different families went all around looking for, you know, do you need me?

17:44.894 --> 17:52.156
And I had essentially given up.

17:52.517 --> 17:55.878
I started making reservations to go back.

17:57.302 --> 18:08.107
And I got one of my friend who was the retired Air Force officer, Colonel.

18:09.931 --> 18:13.594
called me and said, I got somebody, but you gotta help.

18:13.914 --> 18:15.635
I said, okay.

18:16.495 --> 18:29.143
Turns out it's a junior college, and their math professor, a lady, turned really sick.

18:30.984 --> 18:36.387
And I said, well, we need somebody to teach you to.

18:37.073 --> 18:45.542
calculus one and two right okay uh i haven't done that but i did study that way back and so

18:50.633 --> 18:57.360
He was the president of the college.

18:57.380 --> 19:02.526
He wrote a letter and said, take that to immigration.

19:02.926 --> 19:06.190
And the same lady said, well, OK, I'm impressed.

19:06.210 --> 19:06.711
So now I had

19:11.335 --> 19:17.317
And that was valid for two years, I think, yeah.

19:18.997 --> 19:29.080
And so, and as I said, I had met my wife there, which, so I have now all kinds of reasons for being nice.

19:29.520 --> 19:32.121
I asked her, it turned out she's from Germany.

19:33.323 --> 19:36.025
which is interesting.

19:36.165 --> 19:40.328
She's much younger than I am.

19:40.748 --> 19:50.094
So we were the start of Europe.

19:50.934 --> 20:02.462
When we decided we got serious with each other, we went to Europe to meet our respective in-laws.

20:05.735 --> 20:13.541
My mother had lots of friends, a lot of friends.

20:13.561 --> 20:20.187
She was working as a translator for German and English to French.

20:21.968 --> 20:31.717
And I knew several of her friends were Jewish and had serious problems during the war.

20:33.028 --> 20:36.269
And here I come with a German girl.

20:36.309 --> 20:40.390
And I thought, well, wait, she's much younger than I am.

20:41.111 --> 20:50.734
That certainly wasn't her fault, but even she was born after the war was over.

20:51.254 --> 20:57.396
So I had all kinds of reasons prepared, but I had forgotten how to

20:58.061 --> 21:01.223
French, especially the Parisians, think.

21:02.243 --> 21:04.825
And they didn't say, but she's German.

21:05.125 --> 21:09.147
They said, wait, she isn't French.

21:09.327 --> 21:13.170
I wasn't prepared for that.

21:13.510 --> 21:19.893
Yeah, well, I didn't go to the U.S. to specifically look for a French girl.

21:20.754 --> 21:21.714
That was funny.

21:21.794 --> 21:25.336
But we got along with our respective in-laws.

21:29.441 --> 21:37.930
So after the two years in the junior college, I decided to go back to school.

21:38.070 --> 21:47.199
So I got a student visa, University of New Hampshire, electrical engineering, and I got bachelor's and master's from there.

21:48.020 --> 21:49.201
And in the process,

21:50.458 --> 22:01.465
somebody say, you know, you're legal with your student status, but you've been here for so long, why don't you try applying for a green card?

22:03.006 --> 22:04.627
And so I did.

22:06.928 --> 22:12.472
And it's when I was a senior at the university,

22:13.303 --> 22:19.852
I got a letter saying, you got the, okay, we've got the green carpet, you gotta go register for the draft.

22:23.135 --> 22:27.778
Oh, the middle of the Vietnam War, right?

22:28.318 --> 22:30.079
So I went there.

22:30.259 --> 22:37.963
And I still remember the master sergeant who was interviewing me.

22:38.023 --> 22:44.767
He said, yeah, OK, so what's your background, et cetera, et cetera.

22:44.787 --> 22:47.869
I said, you know, I just did three years of military.

22:49.048 --> 22:51.470
He said, wrong army buddy.

22:53.131 --> 22:54.091
Yeah.

22:54.471 --> 22:55.672
Yeah.

22:56.252 --> 22:57.613
So sign up here.

22:58.453 --> 23:09.459
And then toward the end, because then I had to take a physical and this, and then I came back to him and he said, you forgot to write your birth date.

23:10.760 --> 23:11.540
Yeah, sorry.

23:11.740 --> 23:14.302
He said, wait, you're over 27.

23:16.172 --> 23:40.924
uh so you're right we need you to register but unless we declare officially a war you know you're too old yeah okay i guess that's accomplished so and uh so i about well several months later i got the green card and oh dang

23:42.459 --> 23:45.500
You get to serve in one army and then almost.

23:46.380 --> 23:47.441
Yeah.

23:47.501 --> 23:47.741
Yeah.

23:47.781 --> 23:50.182
And I wasn't, I had to think about that.

23:50.322 --> 23:55.263
You don't really want to go to Vietnam and fight over there.

23:55.963 --> 23:58.044
Well, yeah, I think it's worth it.

23:58.104 --> 24:04.106
Besides, my wife had no interest in going back to live in Germany.

24:05.146 --> 24:06.567
So she stayed here.

24:06.587 --> 24:08.087
I think I'm staying here.

24:08.107 --> 24:08.607
Yeah.

24:08.888 --> 24:09.728
It's impossible.

24:11.129 --> 24:12.689
What was, did your wife work?

24:13.270 --> 24:26.933
Yeah, she, well, yeah, when I met her, she was working in the pharmacy, not as a pharmacist, but to help the customers and things.

24:29.014 --> 24:35.876
And then when I decided to go to university, she, for some reason, followed me.

24:38.345 --> 24:45.750
And she worked in the pharmacy there, but she was working part-time and going to secretary school.

24:45.870 --> 24:46.710
Wow, okay.

24:50.313 --> 24:51.633
So she was also really busy.

24:51.673 --> 24:53.695
She had a green card already.

24:53.795 --> 24:54.855
Oh, okay.

24:54.915 --> 24:59.898
She came as, you know what au pair?

25:00.399 --> 25:07.143
She was for an American, it was a major, I think.

25:07.983 --> 25:19.830
for whom he babysitted his kids while he was stationed in Germany and done other things.

25:19.990 --> 25:22.472
And so they knew each other very well.

25:22.592 --> 25:26.114
And he said, come on over.

25:27.435 --> 25:33.699
And then she was au pair with them for, I don't know, three years or something like that.

25:33.939 --> 25:35.080
Pretty good amount of time.

25:35.120 --> 25:35.360
Yeah.

25:37.868 --> 26:03.008
so university uh yeah bachelor's master's and then i worked for what was named general telephone which is now uh verizon in in massachusetts just outside of boston

26:04.774 --> 26:09.556
I was with them for five, five years.

26:11.277 --> 26:15.958
And then did something I wanted to do for decades.

26:16.058 --> 26:19.600
I wanted to go overseas and help the people.

26:23.341 --> 26:28.903
But I mean, I'm not, you know, I'm not a doctor and I'm not.

26:29.023 --> 26:34.125
So, but if they need help with,

26:35.376 --> 26:38.938
maybe I can do training and things like that.

26:40.299 --> 26:49.586
And so I had applied to the UN as a specialized agency called the International Telecommunication Union.

26:50.547 --> 27:03.377
And one branch of that is development for less developed countries.

27:05.982 --> 27:16.749
So I applied, I had applied already coming out of the radio school and they said, well, we don't take people at the technician level.

27:20.332 --> 27:25.396
So once I got the actual degree, I reapplied and they said, yeah, that's good.

27:28.678 --> 27:33.962
But we prefer, since you're going in those countries,

27:34.832 --> 27:40.257
you really needed another, I mean, a higher degree.

27:40.357 --> 27:42.619
And it turned out they were absolutely right.

27:44.681 --> 27:58.754
In most of these countries, people will not take, I don't want to call it orders, but directives from somebody who is not at a higher level than they are.

27:58.894 --> 28:00.616
And they were absolutely right.

28:01.778 --> 28:06.743
So, okay, I got my master's and I applied again.

28:07.844 --> 28:09.006
And it's very good.

28:09.286 --> 28:17.675
We'd like to have you, but since you're going to be the specialist over there, they call it an expert, which is unfortunate because then you go there and...

28:18.563 --> 28:21.206
Well, you're expert in everything telecom.

28:21.226 --> 28:24.209
Well, no, I never worked at those guys.

28:24.229 --> 28:25.810
They say they were there.

28:26.551 --> 28:42.687
Anyhow, so you have some experience in the military and some, but again, you need to have a lot of experience, otherwise they're not going to listen to you.

28:44.394 --> 28:46.642
So five years, ooh.

28:47.304 --> 28:50.355
So that's where I went to work for General Telephone.

28:51.402 --> 28:57.005
So after, and while I was there, after the five years, I reapplied.

28:57.045 --> 28:58.566
No, I actually didn't.

28:59.307 --> 29:11.914
I got a letter from the ITU saying, we revised your, not revised, we reviewed your application, and we noticed you got five years now.

29:12.835 --> 29:19.379
We'd like to have you, there are three possible positions for you, and one was Equatorial Guinea,

29:21.753 --> 29:27.857
And, you know, as the name indicates, they're right on the equator, right in Africa.

29:28.718 --> 29:36.363
And so we think temperature and humidity here is bad, but over there, and they don't have air conditioning.

29:36.883 --> 29:42.327
People were telling me, you know, we changed shirts at least twice a day, if not three times.

29:43.607 --> 29:46.208
And I didn't want to go there alone, right?

29:46.268 --> 29:51.290
So by then we had, the twins were born, so no.

29:51.850 --> 29:53.311
Okay, what's the other one?

29:53.431 --> 29:58.753
Saudi Arabia, with my wife and two daughters?

29:59.673 --> 30:00.293
No, sorry.

30:01.174 --> 30:03.614
Well, we have one in Jamaica.

30:04.495 --> 30:06.315
Ah, that sounds good.

30:08.554 --> 30:10.856
So I took that job.

30:10.916 --> 30:12.176
That was for two years.

30:12.217 --> 30:14.298
They asked me to stay for an extra year.

30:15.299 --> 30:34.071
Then as I was ready to come back to the U.S., my boss's boss in Geneva, where the special agency is, actually came and said, we started something in Haiti.

30:34.792 --> 30:36.613
We need somebody who speaks French.

30:38.963 --> 30:41.045
Well, I think I qualified with that.

30:43.086 --> 30:44.947
But Hayden?

30:45.627 --> 30:46.188
Yeah.

30:46.448 --> 30:50.850
He said, hey, how about take your wife and kids and come for a while.

30:50.890 --> 30:54.172
You help us write the proposal.

30:54.192 --> 30:58.154
You know, they can go around.

30:59.755 --> 31:05.499
Well, and that was during Baby Doc, the dictator there.

31:06.918 --> 31:11.820
But Jamaica had its own problem.

31:13.600 --> 31:18.982
The two major political parties, and they were literally fighting each other.

31:19.002 --> 31:22.583
They left foreigners alone, which

31:33.993 --> 31:43.076
there were a whole region of Kingston, the capital of Jamaica, where, you know, just don't go there.

31:44.317 --> 31:56.681
So we get to Haiti, and I mean, wait, what's going on?

31:56.781 --> 31:58.122
It's so calm here.

31:59.433 --> 32:01.314
Of course, I found out later why.

32:01.394 --> 32:09.598
He'd get out of, you know, maybe the full dictatorship, so you don't mess around.

32:10.099 --> 32:19.684
But I remember walking with him one evening, and we kind of lost our way, and we were in the harbor area.

32:19.784 --> 32:21.685
And I thought, oh, that's not a good idea.

32:22.485 --> 32:25.687
And people came to ask us,

32:26.575 --> 32:31.188
not for money or, you know, what the heck are you doing here, but are you lost?

32:31.228 --> 32:32.071
Can we help you?

32:33.365 --> 32:35.406
That's different.

32:35.426 --> 32:37.947
So based on that, many other things.

32:38.007 --> 32:40.108
The job was very interesting.

32:42.469 --> 32:57.715
So after discussion with my wife and then three daughters, we went there for three years, which ended up being six.

33:00.432 --> 33:04.234
So did a lot of training and organizations.

33:07.255 --> 33:13.979
And then my next post was going to be, still with the UN, was going to be in Brazil.

33:15.800 --> 33:24.024
And I told my then, he was my direct boss, and I was in Geneva because I was project manager.

33:25.259 --> 33:27.160
And I said, I don't speak Portuguese.

33:27.240 --> 33:30.001
I said, you're going to a research center.

33:30.242 --> 33:32.283
They all speak English.

33:32.343 --> 33:33.303
Good.

33:33.423 --> 33:35.104
But wait a minute.

33:36.685 --> 33:41.267
Can I have some details of the different research center?

33:41.467 --> 33:43.268
Yeah, telecom research center.

33:43.288 --> 33:44.448
They're pretty advanced.

33:45.289 --> 33:46.409
They have PhDs.

33:49.403 --> 33:50.703
What do you want me for?

33:51.104 --> 33:52.504
I don't have a PhD.

33:52.564 --> 34:00.367
And I said, look, we've got all kinds of people, researchers.

34:00.727 --> 34:04.948
What we need is something to keep them together.

34:05.069 --> 34:07.049
And we need a team leader.

34:07.489 --> 34:10.550
You've been the project manager there for three years.

34:13.031 --> 34:14.612
OK, if you say so.

34:15.332 --> 34:17.293
So talk to my wife.

34:21.473 --> 34:21.673
No.

34:22.194 --> 34:25.355
By that time the twins were 17.

34:26.575 --> 34:31.017
We are going back to the U.S. You can go where you want.

34:31.237 --> 34:33.758
Okay, at least get overruled.

34:34.998 --> 34:43.461
So we came back to the U.S. And well, it's time to look for a job.

34:43.581 --> 34:45.382
And one of our

34:48.685 --> 34:57.472
Specialized magazine, the IEEE, the Institute of Electronic and Electrical Engineering.

34:57.512 --> 34:59.914
They have many magazines.

35:01.154 --> 35:14.745
And I looked at the job openings, the opening in Texas here, and I was like, hmm, for telecom specifically.

35:15.858 --> 35:16.878
That's unusual.

35:19.739 --> 35:20.480
That sounds good.

35:22.880 --> 35:27.942
So we came and visited.

35:29.843 --> 35:36.045
I got interviewed and I liked them.

35:37.045 --> 35:41.847
So I told them, I had to convince all my

35:46.087 --> 35:53.294
And one argument I use is to say, look, this is a nine-month contract, so I've got three months of vacation.

35:53.394 --> 35:55.416
Think of all the time we're going to spend together.

35:56.356 --> 35:58.619
Forty years later, they still remind me.

35:58.659 --> 36:01.061
So when are we spending all this time together?

36:02.398 --> 36:08.002
I'd say, yeah, it's a nine-month contract, but you do have 12 months of work, right?

36:08.362 --> 36:13.285
But I mean, I don't regret it at all.

36:13.385 --> 36:14.766
Right.

36:15.067 --> 36:21.411
Admittedly, if I had known ahead of time, I probably wouldn't have taken it.

36:23.572 --> 36:25.494
But I'm glad I did.

36:25.534 --> 36:26.995
Yeah, absolutely.

36:27.815 --> 36:30.117
It's, I mean, that...

36:31.775 --> 36:35.376
So, 19 years, 19 and a half years here.

36:36.316 --> 36:36.997
Wow.

36:38.857 --> 36:40.678
You've been everywhere, it seems.

36:41.678 --> 36:53.962
The summers were, you know, taken, well, one thing, as I said, telecom, technology was increasing and improving very quickly.

36:54.910 --> 37:03.716
So one part of my summer is to catch up with this, because I should teach them, and that takes a long time.

37:04.817 --> 37:14.083
Then the books are, by definition, at least a year behind, usually two, because it takes a year to write it.

37:19.688 --> 37:30.825
I put my own notes based on what I had learned in conferences and in specialized magazines and get copyrights and things like that.

37:32.083 --> 37:34.184
that takes a long time.

37:34.244 --> 37:39.665
Then you have to find a way to get scholarship for these people.

37:40.346 --> 37:42.026
You can't do that during the year.

37:44.427 --> 37:47.648
And then also equipment for the lab.

37:48.168 --> 37:56.190
So the summers were pretty busy.

37:56.290 --> 37:59.892
My wife would often say, no, wait a minute.

38:00.212 --> 38:01.132
Industry, you had

38:02.620 --> 38:05.702
weeks guaranteed, right?

38:06.503 --> 38:08.464
And here you can't?

38:08.484 --> 38:15.409
I said, well, no, I've got to do that.

38:15.829 --> 38:26.496
I figured with all the summer months where I was either not paid at all or a half salary,

38:28.877 --> 38:32.339
I essentially worked nine and a half months for free.

38:32.699 --> 38:36.201
Not consecutively, but over the... Wow.

38:37.082 --> 38:39.503
Which, again, I don't regret.

38:40.803 --> 38:53.510
When I left, we had three labs, telecom labs, with equipment, a couple million dollars worth of equipment.

38:53.950 --> 38:55.031
When I came, there was...

39:06.532 --> 39:06.772
there.

39:07.672 --> 39:10.253
That also takes time, by the way.

39:11.374 --> 39:29.600
But, you know, I had several offers from industry as I went along, and so obviously I'd stayed, and I guess I thoroughly enjoyed the relationship with the students, and I felt, you know, former students, some of them still

39:31.166 --> 39:39.511
email me or we meet somewhere and they're grateful and we thank you so much.

39:40.592 --> 39:42.493
I thought you hated my test.

39:42.853 --> 39:46.355
I did, but they were very useful.

39:47.356 --> 39:50.358
There's something to be said about that.

39:50.578 --> 39:54.220
I really don't regret it at all.

39:57.855 --> 39:58.836
So that's good.

39:59.176 --> 40:01.318
Absolutely.

40:01.338 --> 40:09.264
So the process of, because you said you came and there wasn't like a full program, so you helped build the program, essentially.

40:09.284 --> 40:10.825
Yeah, that was my role.

40:10.845 --> 40:11.506
That was my,

40:12.415 --> 40:29.027
I guess it was official title in the department, I don't know if it was true in the college, but I was telecommunication course leader because I was developing all those, which is fine.

40:30.568 --> 40:39.134
And I started as an assistant prof, but of course then you got to do a lot of research and publishing.

40:40.575 --> 40:50.820
And I was reminded of that by the, by the department head, right?

40:52.421 --> 41:03.367
Several of them over the, and I said, we love you here and you're doing your job, but you've got to do research it.

41:04.702 --> 41:06.143
That's not what I came for.

41:06.203 --> 41:07.323
I came for teaching.

41:07.663 --> 41:11.285
Research, I can get a lot better funding in industry.

41:12.365 --> 41:16.207
And they said, yeah, but I can't keep you as assistant prof.

41:17.287 --> 41:21.949
I said, well, any alternative?

41:22.269 --> 41:28.852
And I reminded them, you know, this is an accredited program, and I think I had something to do with it.

41:28.912 --> 41:32.114
He said, absolutely, but the rule is, which is true,

41:33.760 --> 41:37.402
And he said, well, you can be a lecturer.

41:37.762 --> 41:39.582
I was a lecturer in grad school.

41:39.602 --> 41:43.004
How about senior lecturer?

41:45.145 --> 41:45.725
Let me check.

41:46.105 --> 41:48.386
And he came back from the dean and said, yeah.

41:48.998 --> 41:49.979
Okay, you're on.

41:51.320 --> 41:52.401
All right.

41:52.421 --> 41:56.203
But you realize you can never get tenure.

41:56.804 --> 41:58.645
I didn't come for that.

41:58.705 --> 42:03.389
If I'm in the ministry, they can kick me off in two weeks, right?

42:03.969 --> 42:07.852
Here, I've got at least at the end of the semester.

42:09.273 --> 42:10.314
That's very true.

42:12.035 --> 42:14.958
I didn't realize that you would still have to

42:16.678 --> 42:17.318
do research?

42:17.578 --> 42:19.039
Oh, yeah.

42:19.079 --> 42:23.100
If you want tenure, there's no research and publishing.

42:23.180 --> 42:26.661
You've got to publish books, or at least articles, and preferably books.

42:27.821 --> 42:43.725
And I had started writing a book on testing telecommunication equipment because, well, I've used those, I've seen those, but

42:45.583 --> 42:47.804
So the students need it when they get that.

42:49.084 --> 42:51.645
But that takes a lot of time.

42:52.105 --> 42:59.987
And after the second chapter, I had already contacted the editor who was interested.

43:00.768 --> 43:09.430
I said, you know, I know I told you I would do that, but it's either I continue with that at the expense of the students.

43:09.570 --> 43:10.731
You can't do that.

43:11.571 --> 43:14.872
So that was another reason why

43:17.738 --> 43:26.571
And okay, from a, from a, what's the right word?

43:30.979 --> 43:40.227
Sure, if you are an assistant associate prof, that sounds much better than senior lecturer, but who cares?

43:40.867 --> 43:44.310
This is not what I came here for.

43:44.971 --> 43:48.674
So you really enjoy the teaching aspect of it?

43:48.734 --> 43:50.475
Oh yeah, totally.

43:51.096 --> 43:52.357
Yes, very much so.

43:54.808 --> 43:59.733
Again, you know, I had close up with the telecom.

44:01.395 --> 44:10.263
Typically, I had in each course something like between 20 and 25 students.

44:10.323 --> 44:14.948
So you can know that you can relate both ways.

44:16.522 --> 44:24.288
And the, in fact, I told the seniors the first time, I said, okay, now that you're seniors, you can call me Pierre.

44:24.929 --> 44:26.110
And they loved that.

44:26.950 --> 44:35.797
One of my, yeah, I think his title was coordinated.

44:35.877 --> 44:38.559
It's an assistant department.

44:38.619 --> 44:39.180
Okay.

44:40.341 --> 44:44.424
Who somehow one of the students mentioned that, you know, they go,

44:45.502 --> 44:52.846
And he was a retired Air Force guy, recently retired.

44:52.866 --> 44:54.747
And he said, that's not acceptable.

44:56.568 --> 44:58.809
And he told the students, don't ever do that again.

44:58.849 --> 45:04.532
Because students came and said, well, let me go talk.

45:05.052 --> 45:06.973
And he gave me a hard time.

45:07.133 --> 45:10.115
I said, you're going to lose respect for it.

45:12.040 --> 45:18.384
The only reason they respect me is if I'm called by my last name, then I, you know.

45:18.464 --> 45:23.728
They said, no, but it's just not done, which is true.

45:24.148 --> 45:28.410
And then the department had overheard the discussion.

45:28.430 --> 45:32.633
They said to him, he said, you're not in the military.

45:36.487 --> 45:37.548
Yeah, you can relax it.

45:37.568 --> 45:38.588
So that continued.

45:38.868 --> 45:40.990
And the seniors loved it.

45:41.030 --> 45:49.354
That was their graduation to senior level.

45:51.535 --> 46:01.220
When I started, there was an introductory course to telecom for the sophomores.

46:02.417 --> 46:14.625
And then a poorly defined telephone network, I think.

46:16.806 --> 46:21.729
But as I said, that expanded a lot.

46:22.009 --> 46:26.032
I had an industrial advisory board like most programs do.

46:26.724 --> 46:34.547
but who were president or equivalent of telephone companies all over Texas and others.

46:34.927 --> 46:39.189
You know, Southwestern Bell, NGTE.

46:39.429 --> 46:53.155
And when I came at first, the first semester, it's a long, I don't know if I'm diverging too much.

46:55.538 --> 47:18.694
these people involved with telephone companies, I'd ask, I guess the then College of Engineering, I'm trying to remember, well it won't be exact thing, but they said, look,

47:19.212 --> 47:21.474
We need some telecom people.

47:22.095 --> 47:34.065
Sure, you're sending us electrical or electronic people, but then we have to train them for six months because you don't teach this stuff.

47:35.291 --> 47:40.232
the then dean of the College of Engineering, this is 1985, right?

47:41.092 --> 47:50.994
Well, no, in fact, it was before I came, so now he said, oh, but what you want us to do, that's not university level.

47:51.034 --> 47:53.455
That's, you know, that's a technician program.

47:53.475 --> 47:57.996
He said, no, no, we want engineers, but we want a specialized engineer.

47:58.656 --> 47:59.956
Sorry, we can't do that.

48:00.036 --> 48:01.056
We don't do that here.

48:02.877 --> 48:03.697
And so...

48:07.019 --> 48:10.100
I don't remember the details since I wasn't there.

48:10.140 --> 48:11.761
I'm just trying to recollect.

48:11.841 --> 48:20.824
But somebody in the college said to them, said, wait, why don't you try the engineering technology department?

48:20.964 --> 48:23.404
They are not training people for grad school.

48:23.444 --> 48:25.425
They're training people for industry.

48:26.125 --> 48:26.845
So they went.

48:27.046 --> 48:34.288
And so in engineering technology, they said, yeah, we can do that.

48:36.335 --> 48:39.819
So they started that one-and-a-half course I'm talking about.

48:41.321 --> 48:51.034
And so when I came, those same people, presidents of different health workers, came, and we had a meeting.

48:52.521 --> 48:57.925
I introduced myself and said, there are several ways I can orient that.

48:58.446 --> 49:01.008
I did graduate from electronic programs.

49:01.628 --> 49:07.233
Are you interested in them designing circuits or are you interested in them

49:07.773 --> 49:11.135
applying the existing equipment and the best possible.

49:11.715 --> 49:14.696
I'd say circuits we have from electrical engineers.

49:14.736 --> 49:15.877
We got plenty of those.

49:15.977 --> 49:17.258
That's not what we want.

49:17.338 --> 49:20.199
We don't want to have spent six months training everybody.

49:20.979 --> 49:24.681
Oh good, that's where I came from, system side.

49:25.742 --> 49:30.604
And they were, so that I developed the different courses.

49:31.183 --> 49:59.810
based on my experience but also saying okay but what do you people want more specifically what kind of technology are you using and so we started things to respond to that and because they were pleased with the department and with the graduates they started offering some equipment and that's and i remember

50:00.477 --> 50:02.058
That was after several years.

50:02.098 --> 50:08.760
But I kept a relationship with those guys, and we were fairly close.

50:10.501 --> 50:15.323
And I asked one of them, I won't mention the company,

50:17.060 --> 50:22.062
I said, you know, I'm impressed by the amount of equipment you're giving us and the scholarships.

50:22.222 --> 50:26.544
He said, well, there's something special about your program.

50:27.384 --> 50:33.786
We noticed your equipment within a semester or so has been set up and is working.

50:34.847 --> 50:36.867
We know you're doing something with it.

50:37.087 --> 50:42.189
There are other places where that thing just sits there for whatever reason for years.

50:47.753 --> 50:51.954
So, yeah, I mean, and the scholarships too.

50:52.154 --> 51:08.860
And because, well, okay, I get some credit there, because the program was good and correctly oriented, and because telecom was

51:12.260 --> 51:18.042
All my students, if they had C or better, had a job before they graduated.

51:18.082 --> 51:21.544
And some of them went on the internship.

51:21.604 --> 51:30.667
But even besides that, they came here, and they would tell, which didn't please the recruiting folks over there.

51:31.188 --> 51:36.370
They said, no, no, no, we want to talk to the engineering technology telecom group.

51:37.370 --> 51:39.051
But we have many, yes, we know.

51:39.071 --> 51:46.814
Yeah, they, I mean, everybody got a job.

51:46.854 --> 51:47.914
You had to have a seat.

51:47.934 --> 51:49.795
That's fair enough, right?

51:49.835 --> 51:50.795
That's impressive.

51:54.296 --> 52:01.959
One of the petroleum company, they had their own, like all of the large corporations, they have their own telecom network.

52:04.908 --> 52:07.950
So they have a telecom group that takes care of that.

52:09.131 --> 52:16.636
And they're the director of that group.

52:16.736 --> 52:21.118
Now you ask them, you know, I would go and visit them.

52:21.179 --> 52:24.901
That particular thing was in Chicago.

52:24.941 --> 52:32.686
And I asked him over dinner, he said, you know, I'm really grateful that you are hiring my C students.

52:33.586 --> 52:34.207
And they said,

52:35.345 --> 52:46.134
Well, but I've told our recruiting folks, if they come from your program, add a letter to that, then you go up.

52:46.234 --> 52:49.516
So your C's are my B's, and that's fine.

52:50.957 --> 52:51.818
That's high praise.

52:52.038 --> 52:53.820
I like that.

52:53.860 --> 52:54.100
Yeah.

52:54.120 --> 52:54.360
Yeah.

52:56.684 --> 53:00.707
Yeah, it was great.

53:01.728 --> 53:09.895
Well, you know, there's a big satellite antenna next to Thompson that was given to us.

53:12.136 --> 53:15.399
And we used that until I left, I guess.

53:17.841 --> 53:24.208
I hesitate to open the door and see what's inside because I know it hasn't been used since.

53:28.579 --> 53:31.500
Yeah, I mean, it was great.

53:32.321 --> 53:37.623
How did you keep up with, since the technology was changing so quickly, how did you keep up with that?

53:38.003 --> 53:41.285
That was strenuous, I would say.

53:42.765 --> 53:44.286
One thing is conferences.

53:44.386 --> 53:47.948
I would go in the summer and sometimes during the year as well.

53:49.629 --> 53:57.112
Big telecoms, some of them at the national level, conferences and people present their new stuff and they say,

53:59.939 --> 54:01.500
people that can help you.

54:02.780 --> 54:06.922
Yeah, you know, if you have any question of that, just give me a call or do it.

54:08.382 --> 54:11.584
There are a lot of that I didn't know.

54:11.604 --> 54:12.444
Right.

54:12.464 --> 54:13.204
I had to learn.

54:15.285 --> 54:18.006
So that was one main thing.

54:19.487 --> 54:29.271
The other thing is I was, I had the possibility that I could go there and just

54:30.330 --> 54:36.233
spend the day in the company looking around, talking to their engineers and things like that.

54:36.713 --> 54:37.574
That helps a lot.

54:37.634 --> 54:45.898
And they would say, you know, there is this thing, again, that was not internet yet.

54:45.998 --> 54:49.860
We hadn't just emailed.

54:52.201 --> 54:57.484
So, and specialized magazines.

54:57.964 --> 54:58.584
Okay, yeah.

55:00.825 --> 55:03.647
But, yeah, it takes a lot of time.

55:03.728 --> 55:08.732
And some of the stuff says, wait a minute, I better go ask somebody here.

55:08.812 --> 55:13.716
How does that work?

55:14.436 --> 55:20.101
But I'm, you know, I was thoroughly interested in that.

55:21.001 --> 55:22.643
So that, you know, you

55:23.430 --> 55:28.052
you're willing to go deeper and longer than you would otherwise.

55:28.852 --> 55:40.717
I remember talking with one of my colleagues from the English department, and I said, you guys are lucky.

55:43.986 --> 55:46.808
things don't change much, right?

55:47.228 --> 55:50.932
And so you got three months off in the summer.

55:51.372 --> 56:02.983
He said, yeah, but you know how boring it is to teach Shakespeare's to freshmen again and again, and they couldn't care less.

56:04.243 --> 56:12.811
Which is a good point, because since, except the first four years, all the courses I was teaching were at the junior and senior level.

56:14.192 --> 56:18.216
And if they've reached that point, obviously, they're interested.

56:18.336 --> 56:22.781
And that makes it, we had some very interesting discussion.

56:22.881 --> 56:27.425
I used, not every year, but for quite a while.

56:28.249 --> 56:30.771
I had once a week, I had an evening thing.

56:30.811 --> 56:32.412
You guys come and ask questions.

56:33.493 --> 56:37.516
And we bring pizza and just discuss whatever they're interested in.

56:42.500 --> 56:44.181
So I was never bored.

56:45.662 --> 56:46.383
Never bored.

56:47.043 --> 56:55.230
You seem like the type of professor that students would seek out, that they would actually go to, feel comfortable with.

56:55.945 --> 56:59.187
Well, and open office doors.

56:59.307 --> 57:05.630
If I'm there, the door is open, unless there's a student that comes in and has some problem.

57:06.230 --> 57:10.352
He or she doesn't want to, you know, other people are here.

57:10.392 --> 57:12.833
But that helped, too.

57:12.973 --> 57:19.997
And there were many national level conferences, but one in particular

57:21.756 --> 57:31.883
was specifically for the telecom, I think, for the large companies that had their own private telecom.

57:31.943 --> 57:39.488
So there's a whole group of people that you don't, well, the general public doesn't think of.

57:39.568 --> 57:43.311
That's a telephone company or the internet company.

57:43.952 --> 57:49.736
But they have very elaborate networks and they usually,

57:50.565 --> 58:00.388
move with the technology faster than the telephone companies do because of budget and other reasons.

58:02.889 --> 58:12.032
So I almost always went to these groups.

58:13.332 --> 58:18.034
And after a while, in fact, when we became accredited, I got invited

58:18.628 --> 58:20.868
and said, you can bring four of your students.

58:23.449 --> 58:45.853
And so I would pick my four top students, and I told them ahead of time, you know, I'm not going to, this is not a condition, but if I take just my four students in, they can come with me, so they have to pay for the airplane thing,

58:46.645 --> 58:50.607
The conference is free, the room is free, the food is free.

58:50.627 --> 58:51.288
Oh, how fun.

58:52.028 --> 58:59.132
And you're going to be in an environment where you have the most recent equipment.

58:59.413 --> 58:59.793
Yeah.

59:01.274 --> 59:02.174
That helped, too.

59:02.294 --> 59:10.179
And, of course, I would meet these other telecom managers and, oh, we didn't know you had the telecom program.

59:10.199 --> 59:11.139
Yeah, well, yeah.

59:13.035 --> 59:14.716
Yeah, I always, it was good.

59:19.839 --> 59:31.265
When the department offered me a nice retirement reception in the former student and

59:37.270 --> 59:50.356
So the speech and thing, and in mind, I used the occasion to publicly apologize to my daughters and wife for never being there.

59:50.836 --> 59:52.977
Well, never, but that's true.

59:54.498 --> 01:00:03.142
Yeah, there was, I retired two more times, and then they said, well, we're not offering you that.

01:00:03.162 --> 01:00:03.722
No, that was,

01:00:04.630 --> 01:00:09.533
So good, so well done, that was worth three.

01:00:11.353 --> 01:00:16.296
This seems silly that I haven't asked this yet, but how did you first get interested in telecom?

01:00:16.416 --> 01:00:17.977
Ham radio.

01:00:17.997 --> 01:00:19.778
That was definitely...

01:00:24.924 --> 01:00:26.924
Yes, that was the start.

01:00:27.464 --> 01:00:40.627
I started going to meetings, ham radio meetings, and then met others, and then got my...

01:00:40.707 --> 01:00:44.808
In France, you had to build your own transmitter, too.

01:00:45.688 --> 01:00:51.089
But, so, that was... And I had a mentor, if you want, ham...

01:00:55.101 --> 01:01:03.105
And then that's why I went to that radio school after high school rather than wherever.

01:01:04.486 --> 01:01:14.971
How interesting that something, I'm not saying it's simple, but something as simple as a hand radio got you

01:01:16.661 --> 01:01:20.564
all the way from your country, all the way over here, and all these opportunities.

01:01:20.624 --> 01:01:21.385
It's so interesting.

01:01:21.525 --> 01:01:28.690
Well, and that first work permit was the AM radio guide.

01:01:29.030 --> 01:01:37.617
And the, it changed my life, really, for something very good.

01:01:37.637 --> 01:01:37.757
And,

01:01:45.287 --> 01:01:47.989
you come to a brand new place, right?

01:01:48.009 --> 01:01:49.550
Then you know nobody.

01:01:51.991 --> 01:01:56.274
Just pick up your radio and talk through the repeaters.

01:01:56.294 --> 01:01:58.495
Oh, you know, come to our meetings.

01:01:58.595 --> 01:02:11.003
And so suddenly you've got four or five, six people that are their common interest and they know this and that.

01:02:11.783 --> 01:02:14.605
So, yeah, that helped me considerably.

01:02:15.211 --> 01:02:15.631
That's awesome.

01:02:16.092 --> 01:02:16.753
That's so interesting.

01:02:17.474 --> 01:02:31.229
And it's even in the military service that I was, well, I first went to MCO school and became a sergeant.

01:02:31.830 --> 01:02:32.530
But that was...

01:02:34.582 --> 01:02:37.524
in the single core, so in radio.

01:02:38.525 --> 01:02:49.094
And then I was based not far from Paris because I graduated, it was the second day

01:02:49.254 --> 01:02:52.916
or third in line when I graduated from NCO school.

01:02:53.436 --> 01:02:57.178
So they, you had choices, right?

01:02:57.218 --> 01:03:01.860
The first thing, the first guy, okay, what's your preference for the first year?

01:03:01.960 --> 01:03:04.401
After that, we'll send you when we need it.

01:03:05.121 --> 01:03:09.623
And I was like third, or something like that.

01:03:11.104 --> 01:03:13.085
Definitely among the first 10.

01:03:14.925 --> 01:03:30.028
And the guy said, well, sorry, all the places in Germany, because at that time, there was the British, the Americans, the French, but no more space there.

01:03:30.489 --> 01:03:41.111
And there's also, oh, there's a place outside of Paris, that's our telecom training center.

01:03:42.596 --> 01:03:43.456
That sounds good.

01:03:45.757 --> 01:03:51.559
And I didn't tell him that, especially my parents live just across.

01:03:51.919 --> 01:03:54.500
So they're half an hour on bicycle.

01:03:54.520 --> 01:03:55.360
Oh, yeah.

01:03:55.760 --> 01:03:58.721
That's perfect.

01:03:58.761 --> 01:03:59.982
I was lucky there.

01:04:00.402 --> 01:04:03.903
And in fact,

01:04:07.450 --> 01:04:13.593
In that time, military service was mandatory in France.

01:04:14.213 --> 01:04:15.174
So you had to go.

01:04:15.954 --> 01:04:24.978
But they would take you wherever they needed, so you could find yourself, regardless of your background, as a truck driver or whatever.

01:04:25.879 --> 01:04:32.542
But one of the hams in a monthly meeting was a

01:04:34.902 --> 01:04:45.467
At that time, he was either major or lieutenant colonel, but he was, we saw him often.

01:04:45.507 --> 01:04:49.729
He came, I guess he was stationed not far from Paris either.

01:04:50.830 --> 01:05:02.856
And he told me and a friend who was also in the radio school, said, you know, if you want to be in the Civil Corps, the only way that you can be sure of that

01:05:03.868 --> 01:05:08.411
is to enlist rather than wait to be constricted.

01:05:09.191 --> 01:05:11.693
Constricted, yeah.

01:05:13.434 --> 01:05:18.477
So mandatory worth 18 months, year and a half.

01:05:20.239 --> 01:05:22.740
If you're enlist, that's minimum three years.

01:05:24.261 --> 01:05:28.404
But so he said, your choice.

01:05:29.315 --> 01:05:33.116
But I would recommend you do it that way.

01:05:33.396 --> 01:05:41.757
And you also, you know, through your first year, you become a sergeant, which has some advantages.

01:05:43.058 --> 01:05:48.099
And so I talked it over with my friend and said, yep, let's go that way.

01:05:48.219 --> 01:05:54.300
And I remember my dad say, my adopted dad said,

01:05:56.424 --> 01:05:58.686
You're going to get this.

01:05:59.026 --> 01:06:03.369
Yeah, but oh, okay.

01:06:05.470 --> 01:06:09.053
So that was a ham too.

01:06:09.953 --> 01:06:24.884
In fact, the third year, the last year of the service, I was in that training center doing training for people to maintain and repair high power transmitters and things like that.

01:06:26.557 --> 01:06:31.282
And we have a new commanding officer coming in.

01:06:32.683 --> 01:06:38.349
And so, you know, we all wait for him, and he's in speech.

01:06:38.469 --> 01:06:40.871
And I look at him.

01:06:40.931 --> 01:06:42.673
He's in full uniform, of course.

01:06:42.693 --> 01:06:45.155
I thought, I know this guy.

01:06:45.295 --> 01:06:46.997
I couldn't figure out why.

01:06:48.803 --> 01:07:17.416
and that was the guy and he said he called me in his office later we know each other and we're both hands but you do remember that um so um and it's not a personal thing but you know other officers are there so please remember

01:07:23.980 --> 01:07:41.799
but as a reward there is a ham radio station on the floor above my office which has been inactive so you need to get that thing back on the air and use it to call and let people know that this is

01:07:43.048 --> 01:07:46.132
That sounds like a good deal.

01:07:46.934 --> 01:07:51.400
Yeah, I mean, all my life.

01:07:53.102 --> 01:07:58.450
The decision to enlist, that must have been scary, especially considering the time period.

01:08:00.576 --> 01:08:02.397
Yeah.

01:08:02.977 --> 01:08:25.443
Yeah, I mean, there is a, especially now, I mean, I'm 88, so, but when I was still in New Hampshire, I remember a reunion on D-Day, June 4th, I'm sorry, June 6th, 1944.

01:08:27.302 --> 01:08:32.564
And there were several retired officers there, including the one that got me that teaching job.

01:08:33.585 --> 01:08:36.486
And so, well, where were you on your barbecue and things?

01:08:36.546 --> 01:08:38.227
He says, where were you on D-Day?

01:08:38.327 --> 01:08:42.809
I'm in the U.S. somewhere in the Pacific.

01:08:42.849 --> 01:08:53.934
And I said, it comes to me, and I'm at least 10 years younger, if not 15, than they are.

01:08:54.702 --> 01:08:57.483
Well, I guess I was the only one in Norfolk.

01:08:58.363 --> 01:09:02.884
And I remember he said, you're enlisted at age eight?

01:09:02.964 --> 01:09:03.865
You didn't ask.

01:09:04.145 --> 01:09:08.546
I wasn't on the beach, but you asked him, where were you in Norfolk?

01:09:08.746 --> 01:09:09.886
I was in Norfolk.

01:09:09.906 --> 01:09:14.668
I happened to be a seven-year-old kid when you guys came along, and I was glad to see you.

01:09:16.248 --> 01:09:20.990
But that was, yeah, I mean, during the war,

01:09:25.023 --> 01:09:27.964
there were some very close calls.

01:09:28.224 --> 01:09:48.294
In fact, we moved from Paris to the south of France because, I don't know if you know that, but the armistice with the German, the Germans said, okay, we're occupying the northern part of France and the rest is yours.

01:09:48.874 --> 01:09:49.775
I mean, yours.

01:09:49.875 --> 01:09:52.236
I mean, they're both France, but that's under

01:09:52.645 --> 01:09:57.366
French authority, and this is, of course, that didn't last very long.

01:09:57.966 --> 01:10:02.367
So we moved from Paris to the south of France.

01:10:04.268 --> 01:10:13.290
And then when the Nazis occupied the south part, we moved out and went.

01:10:14.030 --> 01:10:21.952
And I kidded my dad for a long time that we went to Normandy, not on the beach, right, on this small village.

01:10:23.103 --> 01:10:27.645
And I kidded him afterwards, said, of all the places.

01:10:28.806 --> 01:10:42.552
And he said, everybody knew that the Allied weren't silly enough to try to scale the Atlantic Wall with all the protection and all the forts and all that.

01:10:42.592 --> 01:10:44.974
They're going to come from England.

01:10:45.794 --> 01:10:51.517
Well, they came from England directly or from the Mediterranean.

01:10:53.343 --> 01:11:00.026
And he's right at that time, including Hitler, unfortunately, he thought theater.

01:11:00.246 --> 01:11:11.430
So it was quiet until about a year before 1944, because then we saw that we were in a small village.

01:11:12.991 --> 01:11:16.392
There was a train line going through the village.

01:11:18.980 --> 01:11:24.226
train with ammunitions and you name it, going back and forth.

01:11:27.410 --> 01:11:37.341
So that was dangerous by itself, but the British Air Force and the American would

01:11:44.575 --> 01:11:50.708
So there were two big hills in the village.

01:11:51.410 --> 01:11:59.033
and they had caves to make Galvados the equivalent of Cognac, if you want.

01:11:59.753 --> 01:12:01.374
So pretty deep.

01:12:01.814 --> 01:12:16.279
So whenever there was an alert or you heard the bombers coming to half of the village go there, and sometimes they, certainly overnight and sometimes days and days.

01:12:16.439 --> 01:12:17.620
And some of the bombs came,

01:12:18.144 --> 01:12:18.884
pretty close.

01:12:19.024 --> 01:12:20.685
I mean, we lucked out.

01:12:22.946 --> 01:12:24.607
It's, yeah.

01:12:27.528 --> 01:12:43.315
And then, you know, the German occupation of the village, and so, you know, they get all the food and, I mean, all the meat and all the goods

01:12:50.710 --> 01:12:56.876
And you never knew what would happen.

01:12:57.116 --> 01:13:06.985
Yeah, I mean, those two years were painful, to say the least.

01:13:07.045 --> 01:13:08.607
And we looked out.

01:13:08.807 --> 01:13:10.769
It could have been much worse.

01:13:12.270 --> 01:13:14.652
I'm glad it turned out for the better.

01:13:16.265 --> 01:13:17.526
Yeah.

01:13:17.686 --> 01:13:36.780
And the village fortunately didn't have any strategic value, but so our village didn't get liberated by the Americans until August something, early August.

01:13:36.800 --> 01:13:41.303
So, you know, the G-Day was June 6th, right?

01:13:42.184 --> 01:13:42.404
Yeah.

01:13:45.458 --> 01:13:48.361
two months, two and a half months before they got.

01:13:48.882 --> 01:13:54.328
And meanwhile, they were fighting everywhere and the Germans were still there.

01:13:57.639 --> 01:14:02.081
Sorry, I didn't mean to bring up anything painful.

01:14:03.401 --> 01:14:22.608
Well, yeah, and then it was, it took me years before I could eat, you know, turnips and whatever, yes, and even carrots, because you eat those every day.

01:14:26.228 --> 01:14:39.777
because the German officers said, you know, the butter, the meat, and what they didn't go to, you know, the NCOs, but not to us.

01:14:42.118 --> 01:14:47.462
Plus, you never know, I mean, there were all the concentration camp business, and you never know

01:14:53.285 --> 01:15:20.305
for you know in a in a way i was lucky because i was only five six seven years old and i didn't realize at that time how you know horrific yeah and i that's one of my regrets i never realized until much much later how much uh my dad

01:15:22.342 --> 01:15:37.357
Having accepted my mother and me through all the stuff that he had to get through, we owe him a lot.

01:15:37.457 --> 01:15:42.563
I think it's very brave of you.

01:15:43.404 --> 01:16:11.448
to you've gone through all of that and then just to enlist i think it's very so sorry that's a personal comment but i think somebody who's gone through something like that and then still faces it head on that's very brave yeah there to get back to the food problem no i'm sorry um it was i was so thin that the war after you know the

01:16:11.889 --> 01:16:32.186
liberation, the Red Cross sent me to Switzerland to go eat, really, for, I don't remember, several, maybe a couple of months.

01:16:32.466 --> 01:16:37.810
I was a farmer that accepted, you know,

01:16:47.159 --> 01:16:48.179
something to do that.

01:16:49.680 --> 01:16:51.461
But yeah, I always know.

01:16:52.981 --> 01:17:02.065
And yet, look, I'm 88, so I guess constitution, physical, was pretty good after all.

01:17:02.085 --> 01:17:03.706
Yeah, you made it.

01:17:04.846 --> 01:17:06.887
Yeah, it's, yeah.

01:17:09.328 --> 01:17:15.951
What were some differences coming over to America, like even culturally?

01:17:18.878 --> 01:17:21.602
Yeah, that was a big change.

01:17:22.683 --> 01:17:26.989
But there's several things that just totally amazed me.

01:17:27.049 --> 01:17:28.751
And I mentioned four, five, six families

01:17:34.207 --> 01:17:35.407
but that was it.

01:17:36.008 --> 01:17:49.313
The Air Force colonel and his family, they kind of adopted me, but the others, and yet they all spent a lot of time trying to find me a teaching job.

01:17:50.834 --> 01:17:52.294
Wow.

01:17:52.435 --> 01:17:59.758
That really amazed me.

01:17:59.838 --> 01:18:02.859
In general, the reception

01:18:03.912 --> 01:18:09.356
from, again, Peterborough is a small town, and that maybe that helps.

01:18:12.038 --> 01:18:16.020
But I was, those people are really nice.

01:18:16.361 --> 01:18:23.305
And, you know, inviting me for barbecues, for this, for family of occasions.

01:18:29.610 --> 01:18:32.432
Of course, the

01:18:45.513 --> 01:18:58.861
Yeah, we... And the guy that was in another, that offered me the second job, that was in another town.

01:19:00.882 --> 01:19:07.245
But I don't think that would have happened in France.

01:19:08.306 --> 01:19:12.748
Ham radio group, yes, but outside of that, these were not hams.

01:19:12.848 --> 01:19:13.749
My boss was.

01:19:16.113 --> 01:19:27.959
And another thing that I wasn't paid very much, understandably, but enough to buy a second-hand car.

01:19:29.360 --> 01:19:31.060
It was old, but it was working.

01:19:31.521 --> 01:19:33.962
Whoa, that's great.

01:19:37.212 --> 01:19:42.976
People don't believe me when I tell them, but the gasoline was 25 cents a gallon in 1964.

01:19:43.056 --> 01:19:49.400
So, of course, we were...

01:19:51.181 --> 01:19:53.262
So I was able to go around.

01:19:53.562 --> 01:19:54.363
Yeah, my...

01:19:56.842 --> 01:20:03.888
My work permit didn't allow me to leave New Hampshire, but New Hampshire was pretty good and pretty good.

01:20:05.429 --> 01:20:10.152
And the people were very friendly.

01:20:10.973 --> 01:20:26.165
Another thing later on, when I started working for General Telephone, we bought a house, not right away, but, and

01:20:28.004 --> 01:20:30.506
So I was an engineer, master's degree.

01:20:31.847 --> 01:20:34.770
And the guy next to me was a truck driver.

01:20:34.990 --> 01:20:36.872
And I thought, that's amazing.

01:20:37.072 --> 01:20:40.595
It could never happen in France.

01:20:40.755 --> 01:20:45.059
A, because, I mean, there's a social structure.

01:20:45.079 --> 01:20:47.421
You couldn't be just a truck driver.

01:20:48.161 --> 01:20:50.944
And the guy was very nice, by the way.

01:20:51.484 --> 01:20:53.266
But I thought, that's fantastic.

01:20:53.826 --> 01:20:54.427
It is a...

01:20:58.398 --> 01:21:06.446
On the other hand, so it's not the social status, it's the salary that we were paid.

01:21:07.748 --> 01:21:11.691
Yeah, obviously made good salary.

01:21:12.252 --> 01:21:19.559
So people combine based on their financial status rather than social status.

01:21:20.120 --> 01:21:21.361
But I kind of like that.

01:21:24.324 --> 01:21:27.506
Now that you point that out, I guess that has a lot to do with the government.

01:21:28.547 --> 01:21:28.907
Maybe?

01:21:29.027 --> 01:21:29.767
Maybe the government?

01:21:29.787 --> 01:21:32.229
Well, the history, I think.

01:21:32.309 --> 01:21:43.475
I mean, France, Paris was, when I was there, 2,000 years old, and now it's another 200 years.

01:21:44.215 --> 01:21:47.917
There's a whole, yeah, there's a, most of,

01:21:48.430 --> 01:21:53.753
Western Europe, you know, England included, you know, there's a social thing.

01:21:55.214 --> 01:22:08.420
Which is another thing that ham radio helped, because during those meetings there was no, you didn't talk about, you know, I'm, you know, Dr. So-and-so, you know, which was ham radio.

01:22:09.821 --> 01:22:11.562
So that was true in France, too.

01:22:13.770 --> 01:22:42.343
No, there were a lot of, and the opportunities later on, it, yeah, in fact, of course now it wouldn't work either, but I was going to say, in France, without a PhD, you can't be teaching at the university, period.

01:22:43.896 --> 01:22:59.980
Here, that's true now, but because engineering technology was not preparing people for grad school, they say, okay, you don't have a PhD, but you've got 10 years of industrial experience.

01:23:00.080 --> 01:23:02.301
So for us, that's actually good.

01:23:03.101 --> 01:23:04.882
They no longer do that.

01:23:04.902 --> 01:23:08.663
In fact, when I, one year I made,

01:23:11.333 --> 01:23:16.236
the outstanding prof for, you know, the College of Engineering.

01:23:16.296 --> 01:23:20.118
Each college has one, and I did it for the College of Engineering.

01:23:20.499 --> 01:23:25.081
So big receptions and speeches.

01:23:26.662 --> 01:23:33.067
And I was, so the ten of us are sitting together on the podium.

01:23:34.640 --> 01:23:50.854
And I hear the lady at that time who was in charge of the faculty, she was reading some letters from my students and compliments and very nice.

01:23:51.795 --> 01:23:56.899
But I was, so that's 19, I'm sorry, 2000, yeah.

01:23:56.959 --> 01:23:57.080
But

01:24:04.477 --> 01:24:10.221
If I applied now, you wouldn't even look at my horizon, because I don't have a doctorate.

01:24:10.641 --> 01:24:16.124
So it's kind of ironic, but in France, there is no exception.

01:24:16.984 --> 01:24:19.586
You can't teach at a university, you don't have a PhD.

01:24:19.726 --> 01:24:20.306
Interesting.

01:24:21.307 --> 01:24:22.367
So that was a plus.

01:24:22.567 --> 01:24:31.852
And even, well, the junior college, they wouldn't require, but a technical school in France, no, there you don't need a PhD either.

01:24:34.177 --> 01:24:49.181
So there's definitely more opportunities here than to think.

01:24:49.241 --> 01:24:56.083
Things have changed in Europe a lot, but in that time, it was much more structured.

01:24:57.643 --> 01:25:02.525
Yeah, that makes sense.

01:25:05.359 --> 01:25:09.242
This life here is very comfortable.

01:25:11.064 --> 01:25:18.690
Lots of, what's the right word?

01:25:19.631 --> 01:25:22.894
Lots of stuff you couldn't do elsewhere, I think.

01:25:24.555 --> 01:25:27.157
It's a bit frenetic.

01:25:27.197 --> 01:25:31.821
There is something in France and the other Western European countries

01:25:32.383 --> 01:25:34.144
You have five weeks of vacation.

01:25:34.925 --> 01:25:37.086
Paid.

01:25:37.446 --> 01:25:41.148
So you've got a lot more time to spend with your kids.

01:25:43.089 --> 01:25:46.031
That I missed.

01:25:46.752 --> 01:25:47.612
And you could travel.

01:25:47.652 --> 01:25:55.457
I mean, I used to travel with my parents until I was 21 or something like that.

01:25:55.897 --> 01:25:57.498
We would still go together because

01:26:03.186 --> 01:26:22.280
There's no perfect place, but I'm certainly grateful to this one, which is why I joined the Coast Guard Auxiliary, which is the volunteer branch of the Coast Guard, and I just completed my 25th year with that.

01:26:22.300 --> 01:26:25.042
That was my way of saying thanks.

01:26:26.283 --> 01:26:27.784
here, maybe I can help.

01:26:27.884 --> 01:26:30.307
Yeah, still, wow.

01:26:30.447 --> 01:26:34.970
Yeah, I got my fourth golden star.

01:26:35.551 --> 01:26:40.475
The ribbon is the first five years, and each gold star is another five years.

01:26:41.676 --> 01:26:45.479
Yeah, that, in fact, I, yeah.

01:26:47.701 --> 01:26:48.702
So I still do that.

01:26:48.722 --> 01:26:54.467
Of course, I don't go on patrols anymore, boat patrols anymore, and

01:26:56.729 --> 01:27:05.361
stuff because I don't have a strain and I have usual age problems.

01:27:06.622 --> 01:27:13.411
But I'm still a member, I'm still their communication officer for some strange reasons.

01:27:15.380 --> 01:27:26.871
I was the HR officer for the division level for, I've retired, I stepped down from that last year because I was forgetting too many things.

01:27:26.891 --> 01:27:33.016
It's embarrassing when you're HR, you deal with all the newcomers, right?

01:27:33.056 --> 01:27:35.899
Then you help, you have to do this and this.

01:27:36.600 --> 01:27:40.123
And then three weeks later, the guy would come back and

01:27:45.857 --> 01:27:48.398
So, but I mean, that's normal.

01:27:48.458 --> 01:28:01.224
But yeah, in fact, we are, we're going to have, our, we call that the flotilla, that's more good, is on Lake Conroe.

01:28:01.244 --> 01:28:12.929
And we're going to have a common maneuver training the end of May, May 30th, together with a regular coast guard.

01:28:14.961 --> 01:28:21.627
They have several rescue and fireboats and the lake constable.

01:28:22.288 --> 01:28:27.493
We do a common maneuver, which is part of training.

01:28:27.513 --> 01:28:29.775
We do search and rescue type thing.

01:28:31.596 --> 01:28:33.958
Normally it's done on Galveston Bay.

01:28:39.472 --> 01:28:41.874
And we, yeah, we're the volunteer branch.

01:28:41.934 --> 01:28:46.578
We don't do law enforcement, but everything else.

01:28:48.900 --> 01:29:00.590
Public education, boat inspection, we do public affairs, radio communications when needed.

01:29:03.633 --> 01:29:06.956
And essentially whatever the regular Coast Guard

01:29:08.445 --> 01:29:09.246
to do.

01:29:10.688 --> 01:29:18.417
It goes all the way to cooks, I mean chefs that were chefs in their previous life and are retired.

01:29:19.378 --> 01:29:23.523
And in fact that started in our division when the

01:29:28.068 --> 01:29:36.355
I don't think it was the captain, but one of the officers of the regular boat crew said, wait a minute, you have people who know how to cook?

01:29:36.955 --> 01:29:38.476
Yeah, we need them.

01:29:38.556 --> 01:29:39.537
We don't have enough.

01:29:39.597 --> 01:29:45.402
We have boats that cannot go out on shore because the only thing we have are sandwiches.

01:29:47.023 --> 01:29:54.209
And so there we have people that go help them out Galveston.

01:29:57.180 --> 01:30:03.783
but also fewer of them because you now have to go spend a week or two offshore with that.

01:30:05.483 --> 01:30:18.049
But yeah, and they, so we also have people who help them administratively speaking, because they, the regular culture is so busy these days.

01:30:19.562 --> 01:30:38.723
um well both from a uh protection of the coast that's a joke right but blocking uh and and you name it and that's the role that they used to have which included

01:30:39.785 --> 01:30:42.287
boating safety of the public, right?

01:30:44.169 --> 01:30:47.051
They say, look, we really have less and less time.

01:30:47.151 --> 01:30:48.392
You guys do that.

01:30:48.612 --> 01:31:06.306
So that's why we do patrol in Galveston Bay regularly and we have that on Lake Conroe and many of the lakes to reduce the number of accidents and deaths on the water.

01:31:08.603 --> 01:31:14.229
So since you've gone all over, moved everywhere, do you have anything else on your bucket list?

01:31:14.349 --> 01:31:15.970
Any places you want to visit?

01:31:16.911 --> 01:31:19.454
Well, I've been to quite a few places.

01:31:21.296 --> 01:31:22.096
That's the thing.

01:31:22.156 --> 01:31:26.581
You know, I mentioned the five-week vacation with my parents.

01:31:26.821 --> 01:31:31.265
We could go all over Western Europe.

01:31:32.494 --> 01:31:44.976
I did go to Eastern Europe to what was then Czechoslovakia as part of my job that first year for just a couple of weeks.

01:31:47.117 --> 01:31:50.137
But Prague is a very nice city.

01:31:51.138 --> 01:31:56.919
And my wife is actually from East Germany.

01:32:06.117 --> 01:32:14.901
So we went together with her brother visiting, you know, where they were born.

01:32:15.862 --> 01:32:17.523
And that was interesting.

01:32:17.863 --> 01:32:31.930
In fact, the mayor somehow, I guess her brother just went to the city hall to say, we're here for a visit and we,

01:32:34.741 --> 01:32:38.144
interested and rejoined us and took us around.

01:32:38.264 --> 01:32:42.487
It was really nice.

01:32:43.147 --> 01:32:46.450
Yeah, I've been to lots of places.

01:32:47.250 --> 01:32:54.135
Also, I was a part-time contractor with

01:32:56.087 --> 01:32:58.970
USAID, which doesn't exist anymore.

01:33:00.151 --> 01:33:05.055
One department of USAID was the Office of Foreign Disaster Assistance.

01:33:05.675 --> 01:33:15.684
They go and help people right after, you know, hurricanes, volcanic eruption, to help wherever needed.

01:33:16.407 --> 01:33:24.772
And of course, one problem, especially the smaller Caribbean islands, is they get wiped out.

01:33:24.912 --> 01:33:28.935
And they have no way to call for help.

01:33:29.355 --> 01:33:31.976
Well, dependent situation.

01:33:32.517 --> 01:33:41.242
So one of the things I did with another ham, who was working for them full-time, is we would go down and

01:33:42.948 --> 01:33:59.332
do a temporary either shortwave radio communication or we had a portable satellite station and set it up so they can talk either to Geneva, the Red Cross, or to Washington for the

01:34:04.500 --> 01:34:05.861
the health organization.

01:34:06.242 --> 01:34:07.382
Doctors Without Borders?

01:34:07.503 --> 01:34:09.444
No, no, I mean the government.

01:34:10.745 --> 01:34:24.775
Well, it's international, not international, multinational, power, something.

01:34:24.975 --> 01:34:27.457
It's at the tip of my tongue, too.

01:34:27.497 --> 01:34:28.338
The H.O.

01:34:28.358 --> 01:34:29.138
is Health Organization.

01:34:29.238 --> 01:34:31.280
Oh, Pan American Health Organization.

01:34:32.502 --> 01:34:40.729
For example, sometimes they would ask USAID, can you send us some guys?

01:34:40.769 --> 01:34:43.851
So, went to Guatemala, do that.

01:34:48.655 --> 01:34:50.396
Didn't do much others.

01:34:50.516 --> 01:34:55.900
Well, Mexico, yeah, we did sailing to Mexico several times.

01:35:01.705 --> 01:35:10.849
Atlantic side, and yeah, a lot of Western, essentially most of Western Europe.

01:35:11.569 --> 01:35:17.732
I have lots of cousins, distant cousins.

01:35:18.853 --> 01:35:27.737
We had one major family, greater family reunion in November of 2000, first year I was retired.

01:35:32.835 --> 01:35:35.277
organized by people over there.

01:35:37.179 --> 01:35:46.005
And we had about 110 or 114 people from nine different countries.

01:35:46.145 --> 01:35:49.428
All descended from the same great-great-grandfather.

01:35:50.309 --> 01:35:55.833
And we were in that, it took place in the town where those grandfathers had lived.

01:35:56.443 --> 01:35:57.104
How incredible.

01:35:57.424 --> 01:36:08.312
And, I mean, there was, we had people, yeah, including a family from Hungary.

01:36:09.233 --> 01:36:14.497
And at that time, in 2000, there was free traveling again.

01:36:14.517 --> 01:36:16.479
They had to go through all kinds of things.

01:36:16.519 --> 01:36:21.543
They drove all the way down to the east of France to join us.

01:36:23.021 --> 01:36:24.863
That's where my family's from, Hungary.

01:36:24.883 --> 01:36:25.223
Yeah.

01:36:25.984 --> 01:36:28.407
And so we have people from Italy.

01:36:29.768 --> 01:36:33.292
Interestingly enough, nobody from Spain.

01:36:33.712 --> 01:36:42.622
Catala is definitely, it's from the northern, northeastern part, the place called Catalonia, in fact, which is a province.

01:36:43.258 --> 01:36:51.683
that in their language, Catalan, Catalan means somebody from Catalonia, so we know we have ancestors there, right?

01:36:51.723 --> 01:36:53.924
But we couldn't go that way.

01:36:53.944 --> 01:37:05.990
So it was strange that we had people from Argentina, we had people from India, lots from England, Germany, Italy, France, of course.

01:37:08.391 --> 01:37:12.894
And I visited a lot of these people because

01:37:15.785 --> 01:37:22.231
That group that organized this reunion, then two years later, well, we go to another country.

01:37:22.712 --> 01:37:27.156
And by that time, I was part of the committee.

01:37:27.977 --> 01:37:31.040
Yeah, traveling, I did a lot.

01:37:31.180 --> 01:37:38.647
The evening to Canada, after I was here.

01:37:39.553 --> 01:37:48.035
Well, one of our daughters lives in Washington State, almost in Canada, on the Pacific Ocean.

01:37:49.535 --> 01:38:07.379
So we have a camper van and we, several times, three times, we've gone there and took our time going and spent a month there and then come back to different routes and, in fact, through Canada.

01:38:08.708 --> 01:38:09.108
Wow.

01:38:10.369 --> 01:38:10.689
Yeah.

01:38:10.869 --> 01:38:13.870
World traveler.

01:38:13.990 --> 01:38:14.431
Let's see.

01:38:14.791 --> 01:38:16.572
Of course, Haiti, Jamaica.

01:38:17.292 --> 01:38:37.481
And while I was there, so I was working for the International Telecommunication Union, but the United Nations,

01:38:41.764 --> 01:38:45.846
I'm sorry.

01:38:45.886 --> 01:38:46.847
No, no, it's fine.

01:38:47.287 --> 01:38:54.391
Anyhow, they also, that's the specialized agency, the UN, that deals with disasters.

01:38:55.691 --> 01:39:07.017
And when I was in Haiti, one of the, usually all the project managers would get together once every two months or so to exchange ideas.

01:39:14.400 --> 01:39:16.960
And he said, oh, you're in telecom.

01:39:17.020 --> 01:39:19.241
We need help in the Caribbean with that.

01:39:19.461 --> 01:39:21.922
I said, okay.

01:39:22.102 --> 01:39:23.983
I got a full-time job here.

01:39:24.143 --> 01:39:27.184
I said, who's your boss?

01:39:27.344 --> 01:39:28.004
Geneva.

01:39:28.344 --> 01:39:30.725
I said, oh, I live in Geneva, too.

01:39:34.855 --> 01:39:45.241
They organized something, and I was allowed to take, I don't remember, four days off a month to go.

01:39:46.682 --> 01:39:57.209
I visited the different islands, all the independent ones, not Puerto Rico and the ones that are still territories.

01:40:03.785 --> 01:40:04.305
need help.

01:40:05.206 --> 01:40:15.072
So I would stay several days in this island and another month in that island, which, yeah, I know the Caribbean very well.

01:40:15.672 --> 01:40:16.933
Wow, what a life.

01:40:17.233 --> 01:40:20.395
You've lived so much and you've seen so much.

01:40:21.536 --> 01:40:27.680
Yeah, I've been, ever since the war stopped, I've been very lucky.

01:40:29.348 --> 01:40:29.868
Interesting.

01:40:31.389 --> 01:40:39.314
I know you mentioned how you were writing some chapters to a book, but I think you should write your own book about places you've been to.

01:40:40.015 --> 01:40:40.695
I'd read that.

01:40:43.036 --> 01:40:44.017
I'd for sure read that.

01:40:44.657 --> 01:40:45.038
Yeah.

01:40:48.179 --> 01:40:49.500
I don't have any more questions.

01:40:49.740 --> 01:40:53.643
Did you have anything that you wanted to talk about that I didn't get to?

01:40:53.663 --> 01:40:55.324
Well...

01:40:56.990 --> 01:41:01.114
Yeah, a couple of things.

01:41:02.255 --> 01:41:20.632
One thing when I did decide to come here is when I thought, oh good, I'm gonna stop all the political problems that you work for the UN and in a foreign country,

01:41:22.052 --> 01:41:24.573
you've got to deal with a lot of different things.

01:41:24.813 --> 01:41:28.815
First, you've got colleagues that are from five different countries.

01:41:28.935 --> 01:41:32.616
We all have our own views of things.

01:41:32.816 --> 01:41:36.558
And those are the five experts.

01:41:37.498 --> 01:41:39.499
And then you've got our counterparts.

01:41:40.219 --> 01:41:40.899
And, of course,

01:41:43.481 --> 01:41:45.122
the whole country, right?

01:41:46.603 --> 01:41:54.067
And there are things that are perfectly okay in the U.S., but not at all okay.

01:41:54.287 --> 01:41:58.129
And you don't have time to learn, you learn the hard way.

01:42:02.611 --> 01:42:07.274
So, I mean, again, you know,

01:42:10.642 --> 01:42:12.544
Not regretting any of that.

01:42:12.724 --> 01:42:16.808
But it's different.

01:42:17.008 --> 01:42:20.531
It's different.

01:42:21.151 --> 01:42:25.935
Haiti in particular is a very poor country.

01:42:27.477 --> 01:42:33.382
And they have a former French colony and the history of slavery.

01:42:35.304 --> 01:42:38.065
and there are things that you just don't do.

01:42:39.125 --> 01:42:53.289
So if you ask something from somebody in a way that can be answered by yes or no, they can't say no.

01:42:53.930 --> 01:42:55.390
Oh, okay.

01:42:55.610 --> 01:42:58.931
Can't say no to a superior, right?

01:42:59.651 --> 01:43:01.232
And especially a foreigner.

01:43:03.953 --> 01:43:06.876
A brown, yeah, white guy.

01:43:07.796 --> 01:43:09.318
So they say, yes.

01:43:11.380 --> 01:43:14.823
And then you said, you finished that.

01:43:15.283 --> 01:43:16.344
I tested it.

01:43:16.404 --> 01:43:17.205
It's not working.

01:43:17.225 --> 01:43:19.847
I said, no, I don't know how to do that.

01:43:21.088 --> 01:43:22.789
But I asked you if you'd done it.

01:43:23.970 --> 01:43:24.271
Yes.

01:43:26.921 --> 01:43:27.981
What's going on?

01:43:28.001 --> 01:43:33.246
So I went to see my colleague, my counterpart, who's an engineer.

01:43:34.066 --> 01:43:38.410
He said, well, no, it's not an acceptable answer.

01:43:39.090 --> 01:43:41.212
So you've got to make sure you ask.

01:43:41.252 --> 01:43:46.776
In fact, one time we were looking for a place for a microwave repeater.

01:43:46.796 --> 01:43:50.660
We were in countryside, jeep, fording rivers.

01:43:52.411 --> 01:43:55.652
And on the way back, I got totally lost.

01:43:55.692 --> 01:44:01.615
There was a lady sitting in front of a hut, literally.

01:44:02.655 --> 01:44:06.837
And I asked her, you know, is this the way to Port-au-Prince?

01:44:07.877 --> 01:44:08.998
Guess what she answered?

01:44:09.678 --> 01:44:10.678
Yes.

01:44:11.179 --> 01:44:17.001
So I go there and we drive for hours, maybe not, but for a long time.

01:44:17.021 --> 01:44:19.442
I said, I don't recognize anything like this.

01:44:21.283 --> 01:44:24.067
Turn around.

01:44:29.715 --> 01:44:36.985
It wasn't my counterpart, but one of the Asian colleagues, he was in training, he was an installer.

01:44:38.272 --> 01:44:40.994
said, do you mind if I ask a question?

01:44:41.275 --> 01:44:42.416
I said, please.

01:44:42.736 --> 01:44:47.000
So he said it in Creole, not in French, but I understood.

01:44:47.380 --> 01:44:50.503
He said, which is the way for Port-au-Prince?

01:44:51.063 --> 01:44:52.624
And I said, that way.

01:44:52.824 --> 01:44:55.006
And I thought.

01:44:55.487 --> 01:44:56.728
And it happened.

01:44:57.088 --> 01:44:59.050
Well, yeah, I should have.

01:45:00.611 --> 01:45:01.292
Cultural differences.

01:45:01.392 --> 01:45:03.394
Ideally, you have

01:45:04.674 --> 01:45:10.817
in advance notice, you got a month to read about, but that's not the way things happen.

01:45:11.658 --> 01:45:16.700
I had one week and a half to make a decision whether I would go to Haiti or not.

01:45:21.943 --> 01:45:24.604
It's, yeah, that was interesting.

01:45:24.644 --> 01:45:26.105
Cultural differences, yeah.

01:45:27.983 --> 01:45:29.724
See, I wouldn't even think about that.

01:45:29.985 --> 01:45:33.007
I would have that problem as well.

01:45:33.087 --> 01:45:41.693
Yeah, it's their history, and they still suffer from it.

01:45:42.073 --> 01:45:56.884
And actually, the other problem is even within a telephone company, the position that you occupy

01:46:02.403 --> 01:46:05.245
really a political assignment.

01:46:06.026 --> 01:46:09.328
So, at least at the higher level.

01:46:09.848 --> 01:46:17.614
So you have a situation where a supervisor, and I pointed out, said, did you see what he did?

01:46:17.674 --> 01:46:17.974
Yeah.

01:46:19.255 --> 01:46:20.035
That's dangerous.

01:46:20.616 --> 01:46:23.358
Yes, but I don't want to give him a hard time.

01:46:24.819 --> 01:46:30.743
Because two months from now, maybe the doc might put him as my boss's boss.

01:46:33.926 --> 01:46:35.567
So, can I tell him?

01:46:35.968 --> 01:46:38.769
Yes, please do.

01:46:38.790 --> 01:46:43.493
So even in those instances, you still have to play politics?

01:46:44.727 --> 01:46:47.149
Yeah, well, so, yeah, you're right.

01:46:47.289 --> 01:46:48.089
I diverged.

01:46:48.149 --> 01:46:48.930
Oh, no, no, no.

01:46:48.950 --> 01:46:54.694
So I thought, I'm through with all this political process.

01:46:54.814 --> 01:46:55.054
No.

01:46:55.775 --> 01:46:57.356
It follows you, yeah.

01:46:58.276 --> 01:46:59.497
That was a surprise.

01:47:00.558 --> 01:47:06.522
Although I've been very lucky with all my department heads.

01:47:08.943 --> 01:47:10.024
Some interesting times.

01:47:10.044 --> 01:47:10.144
Yeah.

01:47:10.945 --> 01:47:11.185
Yeah.

01:47:13.893 --> 01:47:25.398
Again, if I had known that I wouldn't have any vacation, I probably would have said not because I had an offer from industry in San Diego.

01:47:27.099 --> 01:47:33.222
And of course, but that's the way it is.

01:47:34.223 --> 01:47:37.764
I wouldn't have a chance to have this relationship with the students.

01:47:38.044 --> 01:47:39.325
Yeah, true.

01:47:40.560 --> 01:47:45.281
Thank you for meeting me and telling me your story, a bit of your story.

01:47:45.301 --> 01:47:49.502
I'm glad if it could be useful.

01:47:49.683 --> 01:47:50.583
Absolutely, it is.

01:47:50.983 --> 01:47:51.523
Absolutely.

01:47:53.604 --> 01:48:01.046
We have roughly 2,000 interviews from the 60s all the way up to the early 2000s.

01:48:03.382 --> 01:48:10.230
One of them was the director of our library, Cushing, and the other one, Terry Anderson.

01:48:10.250 --> 01:48:10.891
He's still here.

01:48:10.991 --> 01:48:12.313
He's a history professor.

01:48:12.333 --> 01:48:13.154
I don't know if you know him.

01:48:13.454 --> 01:48:15.757
I know the school's huge, but he's still here.

01:48:15.877 --> 01:48:23.746
And so once I got hired and I basically got all of his work that he had, I was like,

01:48:24.798 --> 01:48:29.923
It's so nice to meet you, and I'm the one handling all of your interviews that you've given.

01:48:30.043 --> 01:48:32.285
He's like, oh, no, those things, put them back in the closet.

01:48:33.706 --> 01:48:36.368
Put them back in the closet.

01:48:36.388 --> 01:48:36.708
Yeah.

01:48:36.829 --> 01:48:37.789
Oh, good.

01:48:38.070 --> 01:48:38.390
Yeah.

01:48:39.251 --> 01:48:40.051
Thank you so much.

01:48:40.672 --> 01:48:41.533
Very welcome.

01:48:41.553 --> 01:48:41.773
Yeah.

01:48:42.293 --> 01:48:43.074
Good to help.

01:48:43.274 --> 01:48:43.634
Yes.

01:48:45.236 --> 01:48:46.157
I will be in touch.

01:48:46.317 --> 01:48:48.739
I will make sure you get copies of everything.

01:48:52.910 --> 01:48:55.233
Now your wife sounds like someone I should talk to too.

01:48:55.293 --> 01:48:56.714
She is quite a lady.

01:48:56.794 --> 01:49:00.479
She skipped me for 57 years, that by itself.

01:49:02.140 --> 01:49:04.563
She's the nurse, or was a nurse.

